  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonal temperatures and lower humidity \ncontinue through at least early Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure is centered over the Great Lakes this afternoon \nand pushing SE into the northern Mid-Atlantic. Aloft, an upper \ntrough is located from northern New England to Atlantic Canada, \nwith the trough axis lagging back toward the eastern Carolinas. \nPartly sunny and pleasant this afternoon with temperatures \nranging from the upper 70s along the coast to the lower 80s \ninland. The wind is generally E to NE 5-10 mph, and locally \n10-15 mph along the coast. \n\nHigh pressure settles S into the region tonight. This will \nlikely be the coolest night of the week given rather favorable \nradiational cooling conditions. Widespread upper 50s are \nexpected inland, with localized mid 50s possible. High pressure \nremains over the region Tuesday morning and nudges offshore by \nafternoon. Not as breezy with the high in closer proximity to \nthe region. A few degrees warmer Tuesday with high temperatures \nin the mid 80s inland, with around 80F/lower 80s at the coast. \nContinued pleasant Tuesday night as the high remains near the \ncoast. Low temperatures range from the upper 50s to lower 60s \ninland with mid/upper 60s at the coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High temperatures trend above normal by \nWednesday and will likely by hottest by Thursday. There is a \nchance for thunderstorms late this week as another cold front \napproaches and settles into the region.\n\nThe surface high slides well offshore Wednesday into Thursday \nas strong low pressure tracks across Ontario and the associated \ncold front drops across the Great Lakes. Temperatures will warm\nback above seasonal averages with increasing SW flow, though \nhumidity will be a little slower to recover. High temperatures \nWednesday are forecast to be in the upper 80s inland, with mid \n80s at the coast. Thursday will likely be the hottest day of the\nweek with high temperatures into the lower 90s inland, with \nlocalized mid 90s possible. More humid by Thursday as dewpoints \nrise into the upper 60s/around 70F. A S/SW wind increases \nWednesday into Thursday, with gusts up to 20-25 mph possible \nThursday hence the increase in low-level moisture/higher \ndewpoints.\n\nThe cold front approaches the region Thursday, but it may be \nslower to settle into our region as it becomes aligned parallel \nto the mid-level flow, and potentially does not settle into the\nlocal area until late Thursday night/early Friday morning. \nGiven this, any showers/tstms may be slower to approach the \nregion. The latest NBM has 20-30% PoPs across the far N Thursday\naftn, and 30-50% across the N Thursday evening. A few strong \ntstms are possible, but confidence in severe weather is low \ngiven how far north the parent low is, uncertainty regarding how\nhigh dew points (and instability) ultimately get, and timing. A\nfew additional showers/tstms are possible Friday across far \nsouthern portions of the area, but that will depend on how far \nsouth the cold front gets by early Friday. Behind the cold \nfront, CAA will be lacking so temperatures only cool back to \nnear normal Friday and Saturday with lower 90s potentially \nreturning by Sunday and Monday of next week. Some semblance of a\nfront lingers over the area Saturday with chances of \nshowers/tstms persisting. This boundary gradually washes out \nSunday/Monday with diminishing chances of showers/tstms.",
